# `yo fix <path> --migrate unsized`, the `str` family (decision 49 S2,
# VERDICTS-61 §8, VERDICTS-62 §1): a value `str` type is the legacy alias of
# `&(str, static)`, and the migration writes that spelling at a parameter, a
# result, a typed local, a struct field, an array element and a type
# argument (`ArrayList(str)`). It lists a `sink(s) : str` parameter (a
# reference parameter is not a `sink`). `&str`, `impl(str, ...)`, `Self`, the
# alias name `Name` and a type argument spelled through a binder (`T`) are
# left alone. It writes `static` only where the file may spell it (std and a
# pragma'd file, `main.yo`): in safe code (`safe.yo`) that is E0922 while P12's
# rule is blocked, so the written `str` stays and the alias keeps its meaning
# (issues/retired/2026-11-16-the-str-migration-writes-a-static-origin-that-safe-code-cannot-spell-while-p12-is-blocked.md).
# The second run rewrites nothing, and both files still check.
fix . --migrate unsized
fix . --migrate unsized
check main.yo
check safe.yo
